% A restore back past a setpagedevice makes the retired device current
% again, and painting on it must not follow its released buffer. Run
% against a live device (-d <dev>) by run-device-restore-test.sh: prints
% SUCCESS iff every step holds.
%
% setpagedevice retires the outgoing device, which releases whatever raster
% it was holding. PLRM 6.1 makes the device an element of the graphics
% state rather than a global fixture: a graphics state saved before the
% change still names the retired device, and restoring it makes that device
% current again. The interpreter then paints on an instance whose buffer is
% gone, without the program having done anything unusual -- the four lines
% below are an ordinary page-size change bracketed by save and restore.
%
% The device's recorded width and height survive in its private struct, so
% the bounds checks in its marking methods still pass and nothing between
% the mark and the released buffer stops the write. A released raster has
% to take no marks instead.

/pagesize { currentpagedevice /PageSize get } def

save

<< /PageSize [400 400] >> setpagedevice

% the resize took effect while the saved state is outstanding
pagesize 0 get 400 ne
    { (FAILURE: setpagedevice did not resize the page) = quit }
if

restore

% painting after the restore is the step that reached a released buffer
newpath 0 setgray 10 10 50 50 rectfill
20 20 moveto 60 60 lineto stroke
showpage

% a second page proves the device survived the page it just emitted
0.5 setgray 15 15 30 30 rectfill
showpage

% and the device machinery is still usable afterwards
<< /PageSize [300 300] >> setpagedevice
pagesize 0 get 300 ne
    { (FAILURE: setpagedevice stopped working after the restore) = quit }
if
0 setgray 10 10 40 40 rectfill
showpage

count 0 eq
    { (SUCCESS) = }
    { (FAILURE: operand stack unbalanced) = }
ifelse
